1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,599
The harbor schedule board takes four
seconds to paint. The network is fine. The
JVM is fine.

2
00:00:05,719 --> 00:00:13,080
SQL logging shows dozens of similar
selects and wide entity graphs for a
screen that only needs vessel name, berth
code, and ETA.

3
00:00:13,199 --> 00:00:26,960
JPA performance tuning starts by measuring
that gap — then applying batch size,
entity graphs, and projections on purpose.
Enable SQL logging (or a statement
counter) in a lower environment and
reproduce the board load.

4
00:00:27,079 --> 00:00:40,560
Count statements. Note payload width. Only
then pick a tool. Batch size reduces
chatty lazy loads when you must keep
associations lazy: Look at this java for a
moment. Look at this java for a moment.

5
00:00:40,679 --> 00:00:48,079
When the board touches twenty vessels’
lazy state, Hibernate can load them with
fewer WHERE id IN (...) queries instead of
twenty singles.

6
00:00:48,200 --> 00:01:00,520
Batch size is a blunt instrument — better
than N singles, not always better than one
intentional join. Entity graphs declare
what a use case needs up front: Look at
this java for a moment.

7
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:09,200
The graph tells Hibernate to fetch vessel
and berth with the slots for that board
query — without marking the associations
EAGER globally for every other use case.

8
00:01:09,319 --> 00:01:20,599
Projections shrink what comes back when
the board does not need full entities:
Look at this java for a moment. No managed
graph. No accidental lazy navigation in
the JSON serializer.

9
00:01:20,719 --> 00:01:23,400
Columns the screen needs — nothing more.

10
00:01:23,519 --> 00:01:35,879
Other levers: pagination so the board
cannot ask for a year of slots; read-only
transactions for pure paints; avoiding
Open Session in View when it turns
rendering into a query engine;

11
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:38,959
second-level cache only after you
understand eviction.

12
00:01:39,079 --> 00:01:50,439
Indexes still matter — JPA will not invent
an index on eta for you. Marking every
association EAGER "for performance" often
makes writes and incidental loads worse.

13
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:57,760
Caching entities without a plan recreates
stale berth boards. Micro-optimizing flush
modes before counting SQL is folklore.

14
00:01:57,879 --> 00:02:07,039
Even a tuned board can hide a sharper
anti-pattern: one query for manifests,
then one query per manifest for cargo
lines as a loop touches the collection.

15
00:02:07,159 --> 00:02:15,079
That classic explosion has a name — N+1 —
and it deserves its own walkthrough with a
real loop and a join-fetch fix.
